  "live_records": {
    "q-343368": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343368",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{+}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{f}^{+}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2}=\\frac{0+(-3)}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
