    "q-343379": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343379",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ {x}^{2}-1, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-1.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(-1)={e}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
