    },
    "q-343438": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343438",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-4,0] \\\\ 3, & x\\in (0,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-2,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-2)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4})=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-n\\pi )=(-1)^{n}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=-2,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-2)=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4}) \\\\ -1 & =1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
